7] [17592186411847 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411847 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411847 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411847 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dee10a7b-d9ae-5a81-b136-732cb3456d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411848 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f3c0f520-1723-59a6-8809-23fd43dd82d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411853 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2i \\\\ 1-i \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}2-i \\\\ 1+2i \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10da4895-a162-564f-b4fa-20e4534242ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411853 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(3+2i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{2-i})+(1-i)(\\overset{―}{1+2\\text{i}})+3\\cdot \\overset{―}{2} \\\\  & =(3+2i)\\cdot (2+i)+(1-i)(1-2\\text{i})+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(4+7i)+(-1-3\\text{i})+6 \\\\  & =9+4i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411854 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}(x+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{4}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{9}{16},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1994bb13-7165-5891-a83f-5e7560b1da72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411854 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}y[\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{16}-(\\frac{5}{4})(\\frac{9}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411855 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411855 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411855 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411855 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28e99078-3ec4-5f03-89e8-002bc319270f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411855 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411858 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54a3ad17-4b32-5c5c-9805-6f8326875645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411858 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations in the domain $[0,\\pi )$, so this is the only value of $t$ when the object is stationary." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411859 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4d/4d83f771aa650879909c33d7033e80ca044b7e6f8369e19667e6173aa8a40737.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x+\\sqrt{3y})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58242fe6-4f94-5872-a2af-27a25e52256a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411859 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(0,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(0-2)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{3y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2-2t)+\\sqrt{3(3t)} \\\\  & =2-2t+3\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3{t}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{[2t-{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3[(2(1)-(1)^{2}+2(1)^{3/2})-(0)] \\\\  & =3(3) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411860 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=4xy$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ff06561-72e7-5575-b3f6-d0740b1e717f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411860 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](images/eb/eb8745d968da24a8500bd079638b17bc461ab2d97b93d1d1a7d6d4834365bb5a.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411865 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473787 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186411865 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473788 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186411865 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473789 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186411865 #edb/ref 1069 "q-178271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411865 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411865 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411865 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d807e12-6960-57f6-b74d-aa3fe66f0f76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411865 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{3})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{3})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411867 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411867 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411867 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411867 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411867 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a9c63b9-55aa-5f88-8ff0-b14dd8c3b850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411867 #edb/ref 1074 "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}17 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\begin{bmatrix}-81 \\\\ 81\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=5$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=10$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a726a32f-5782-5170-bbe8-758da98cd984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=5$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=10$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=5$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n| $X=10$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{5}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}+{10}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}]-{8}^{2} \\\\  & =10+60-64 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd343afe-c54e-593d-8dc2-ed57fc5e3a91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =P(X<\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9a66f9263c9009f634668e826f0a84320103589b9c270360d7b2465c71e74592.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}2xz\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea08d184-a351-56ae-9a07-8c580e0db18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/49/49a3333a52a6b7d7451095dbc46ab1009df491c92726436929a6f93cff38db60.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-2x\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dfa0a80bc323401f050d1302dc42c92558d8b566d9b0f985e955d34eb5cc15bd.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-2x\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[x{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=3-2x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3-2x)^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}){[y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(18x-24{x}^{2}+8{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(18x-24{x}^{2}+8{x}^{3})\\,dx & ={[9{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}+2{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =[9-8+2] \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186412664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1045 "Weighted Least-Squares" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186412663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412633 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5ae33ea6-f725-5358-bc56-131c7aa58050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412634 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186412634 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412634 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "088981d3-114e-574b-937a-924f96c1a577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412635 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412635 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412635 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f5c53a9-a2e1-5c97-ae8a-eca40c100135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412636 #edb/ref 1080 "42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412636 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1